WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.060
Britain taxed homes by counting their windows to avoid prying inside, and owners bricked

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:11.100
them shut, leaving cities darker and sicker for generations. This is Unintended Consequences,

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:18.240
episode 104. In the winter of 1797, a London bricklayer received an order to seal eight

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:23.940
windows in a merchants house on cheap side, with fresh mortar and timber. The work took only

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:28.740
a single afternoon, yet the rooms behind the new walls stayed cold and dim for the next

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:34.720
century and a half. The tax that prompted the order had been designed to raise revenue, without ever

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:40.080
stepping across a threshold to count silver plate or furniture. After the glorious revolution,

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:45.860
Parliament needed steady funds to support William III's wars on the continent. Direct income

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:50.860
assessment felt both intrusive and technically difficult in an age without reliable records

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:56.760
of personal wealth. A window count offered an external proxy because larger houses tended to

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:02.540
have more windows, so the number visible from the street could stand in for the owner's means. The

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:07.780
approach also avoided the political resistance that came with any new tax on land or hearths.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:13.980
Lawmakers therefore treated the measure as a practical compromise between fiscal necessity and respect

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:20.480
for domestic privacy. At the time, no one expected households to treat windows as moveable items that

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:26.720
could be removed to change a tax band. The arithmetic seemed straightforward enough. A house with 12

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:32.140
windows would pay more than one with six, yet the collector never needed to know whether the extra

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:38.580
openings lit a drawing room full of imported China or simply a narrow stairwell. Because the tax was

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:44.300
collected at the level of the dwelling rather than the individual, it also side-step disputes over how

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:50.240
many adults or servants actually lived behind each facade. In an era when property boundaries were

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:55.900
often informal and rental agreements changed hands without written deeds, counting pains from the

00:01:55.900 --> 00:02:01.380
pavement looked like the least contested way to generate a predictable annual yield. The window

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:08.300
tax took effect in 1696 and applied to every dwelling in England and Wales. Houses with fewer than 10

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:14.740
windows paid a flat rate. Each additional window increased the liability in stepped bands. Collectors

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:20.380
walked the streets, tallied openings, and left notices. Owners could appeal or alter the count

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:26.540
before the next assessment. Early returns showed the tax-producing reliable revenue with little administrative

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:32.260
cost. Proponents noted that the levy fell more heavily on prosperous districts with your requiring

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:38.020
officials to enter homes. A few voices warned that any tax on visible features might encourage

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:43.760
concealment, yet the concern remained theoretical in the first years of collection. When the first

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:48.980
assessments were completed, the number of windows recorded in London alone ran into the hundreds of

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:54.960
thousands and the treasury recorded the expected inflow within weeks rather than months. Because the

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:00.980
bands were coarse, 10 windows, 20 windows, and so on, many owners quickly realized that dropping

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:06.680
just below the next threshold saved a noticeable sum each year. The collectors themselves had no

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:12.060
authority to prevent alterations. Their job ended once the count was logged and the bill presented.

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:16.800
Owners quickly discovered that permanently closing windows lowered their assessments.

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:22.280
Brick layers and carpenters made steady work filling openings with masonry that matched the surrounding

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:28.420
walls. In dense urban terraces that practice spread rapidly. By the middle of the 18th century,

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:33.380
many working-class districts showed long stretches of blank brick where daylight had once entered.

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:39.060
The loss of light and fresh air compounded existing problems of overcrowding and poor drainage.

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:44.760
Contemporary observers recorded higher rates of respiratory illness in these altered houses,

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:50.080
and later medical writers linked the darkened interiors to the persistence of tuberculosis in

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:55.820
industrial cities. The causal chain ran from the tax rule through the physical adaptation to

00:03:55.820 --> 00:04:01.080
reduced ventilation, which allowed airborne pathogens to linger longer inside living spaces.

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:07.400
Second order effects included children studying by candlelight at midday and families crowding into

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:13.240
fewer usable rooms to keep the remating windows. Over decades, the darkened stock of housing became

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:18.840
normalized. New speculative builders sometimes constructed dwellings with the minimum number of openings

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:24.540
from the outset. The health burden accumulated slowly enough that it never registered as an immediate

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:31.000
policy failure. One can trace the arithmetic further. Each bricked-up window reduced the annual bill

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:37.120
by a few shillings. Yet the same opening, if left open, might have admitted enough daylight to allow

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:43.340
a loom or a workbench to operate without extra candles. In households already balancing rent against

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:49.260
food, the choice between a smaller tax payment and an extra hour of usable light was rarely framed

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:55.080
as a health decision. Over successive generations, the altered houses passed from owner to tenant,

00:04:55.660 --> 00:05:00.720
and the memory of the original window placement faded. The blank wall simply became part of the

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:06.720
building's character. When later reformers measured air quality inside these rooms, they found carbon

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:12.400
dioxide levels rising more quickly once the sun set, because the single remaining window could not

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:19.120
exchange air fast enough for the number of occupants. By the 1840s, public health reformers began documenting

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:24.800
the connection between windowless rooms and disease. Parliamentary inquiries into urban sanitation

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:31.620
cited the tax as one contributor to poor living conditions. The levee was finally repealed in 1851,

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:37.260
yet the bricked-up windows remained in place because reopening them required expense and structural

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:43.700
work. Some landlords later added new openings during renovations, but many older terraces kept their

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:49.440
altered appearance well into the 20th century. The episode illustrated how a fiscal instrument could

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:55.700
leave durable physical traces long after the statute itself disappeared. Repeal did not automatically

00:05:55.700 --> 00:06:02.200
restore daylight. Each new aperture still needed lentils, frames, and glass, costs that fell on owners

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:07.640
who had already adjusted their ledgers around the lower tax band. In some districts, the pattern of

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:12.400
sealed windows survived long enough to shape later building codes that required minimum light wells,

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:17.980
an indirect recognition that the earlier adaptation had become embedded in the urban fabric.

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:24.780
Any tax or regulation that uses an observable proxy invites people to alter the proxy rather than the

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:29.600
underlying behavior. Once physical changes are made to reduce the measured quantity,

00:06:30.140 --> 00:06:35.760
reversing the effects often proves slower and costlier than the original adjustment. Systems that

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:41.080
rely on visible features for assessment therefore need to anticipate how those features can be hidden

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:46.900
or removed. The same principle appears today whenever performance metrics or compliance rules are

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:53.620
tied to easily altered indicators rather than direct observation of the desired outcome. How might current

00:06:53.620 --> 00:06:58.700
environmental or housing policies create similar incentives to conceal rather than improve?

